## 1. Introduction
Over the past decades, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) attract more and more research interest, due to their advantageous characteristics, such as large surface area, high porosity, and tunable structure [1-6]. MOFs display potential applications in various fields, such as gas separation and storage [7-9], catalysis [10-13], sensors [14] and magnetic materials [15]. The special features of MOFs also provide unprecedented opportunities as drug carriers. For example, MIL-53[6], UiO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ [16], ZIF-8 [17], MOF-74-Fe [18], Gd-pDBI [19] and MIL-101 [20] were reported to act as efficient nanocarriers for targeted and precise release.
For practical medical and pharmaceutical applications, MOFs are required to be biocompatible [21]. For example, though MIL100(Cr) and MIL-101(Cr) were proved to be effective for ibuprofen (IBU) loading and controlled release, their application is still limited due to the well known toxicity of Cr [20]. Compared with the reported chromium [22], cadmium [23], nickel [24], cobalt [25] based MOFs, bismuth based MOFs hold great potential in the field of drug carrier, as both metal Bi and Bi based compounds are commonly considered biologically safe and nontoxic [26-29].
Furthermore, Bi-based materials are highly photoactive, which suggests that the electronic structure of bismuth based materials can be modulated by light [30]. This provides an opportunity to control drug release over Bi based materials by light. Compared with the other stimulus that can be used to control drug release, such as pH, heat, magnetism, ultrasound and mechanical force, light is especially attractive, as it can be remotely applied with high spatial precision. Additionally, a broad range of parameters (wavelength, light intensity, duration of exposure, and beam diameter) can be adjusted to modulate the released amount [31].
However, due to the flexible coordination (the coordination number ranges from 3 to 12), and easy hydrolysis of Bi, limited Bi based MOFs are developed as far as we know [29,32]. Nevertheless, our group reported several Bi based MOFs, such as Bi-BTC [33], Bi-mna [34], CAU-7-TATB [35], and Bi-MBA [36]. Recently, Erik Svensson Grape et al. reported a Bi based MOFs named SU-101 using ellagic acid (EA) as the organic ligand, which displays many advantages, such as very low cytotoxicity, good biocompatibility, high chemical stability and suitable particle size for intravenous administration [37-40]. Therefore, SU-101 is highly anticipated to be an efficient drug carrier.
To check above assumption, the application of SU-101 in drug delivery is investigated using ciprofloxacin (CIP) as the drug model. It is found that SU-101 displays high drug loading $(85.8\%)$ for CIP, and the loaded CIP could be released under light illumination (the maximum CIP release ratio is $95.56\%$ . Detailed experiments suggest that the drug release behavior is dependent on light wavelength, which coincides with the light absorption of SU-101. Based on the combined results of experimental and DFT calculations, it is believed that hydrogen bond is mainly responsible for the high loading and efficient release. Light breaks the hydrogen bond between the drug molecule and SU-101, causing the release of the drug. Further investigations suggest that SU-101 is universal for other drugs, such as NOR, AMO, TET and DOX, since all of them can form hydrogen bond with SU-101. This work proves that Bi based MOFs hold great potential as efficient light-sensitive drug carrier.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
The reagents were obtained as follows: ellagic acid $(98\%)$ (EA) was purchased from Shanghai Xian-Dinn Biotech Co., Ltd. Bismuth acetate $(99.8\%)$ , ethanol, glacial acetic acid and NaOH were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. Ciprofloxacin (CIP), norfloxacin (NOR), amoxicillin (AMO), tetracycline (TET) and doxorubicin hydrochloride (DOX) were purchased from Aladdin. All chemicals were used as-received without further purification.
## 2.2. Preparation of SU-101
$0.45\mathrm{~g~}$ EA and $1.14\ {\mathrm{g}}{}$ bismuth acetate were added into ${\mathfrak{s o}}_{\mathrm{~mL}}$ water solution containing $5.4~\mathrm{mL}$ acetic acid, which were stirred for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $15~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ After that, it was centrifuged at $8000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ . Then, the sediment was washed with deionized water and ethanol and dried in an oven at $60^{\circ}C$ overnight [37].
## 2.3. Performance of CIP loading and release
25 mg SU-101 were added into $50~\mathrm{mL}$ CIP aqueous solution, and the suspension was kept stirring in the dark. Firstly, we investigated the effect of CIP concentration on loading performance by using different concentrations of $2.5~\mathrm{mg/L},5~\mathrm{mg/L},10~\mathrm{mg/L}$ and $20~\mathrm{mg/L}$ , respectively. Then the loading kinetics of CIP over SU101 were carried out by stirring for $^{10\mathrm{h}}$ using CIP $(10\mathrm{mg/L})$ . During the adsorption process, $1.5~\mathrm{mL}$ suspension were taken out after a certain time. After centrifugation, the absorption change of the supernate at $270\ \mathrm{nm}$ was checked by a UV–visible spectrophotometer (UV-2550, Shimadzu). The sediment was denoted as CIP@SU-101.
After adsorption process, light-triggered CIP release behaviour from SU-101 in vitro was investigated. A 300 W xenon arc lamp was used for illumination. With a $420\mathrm{nm}$ cut-off filter, visible light can be obtained. With an $800\ \mathrm{nm}$ cut-off filter, near infrared light can be obtained. Monochromatic light with different wavelength were produced using a 5 W monochromator. During the process, the absorption change of supernate at $270~\mathrm{nm}$ was determined by a UV–visible spectrophotometer after a certain time. It is worth mentioning that exposure of the suspension to light irradiation does not cause measurable decomposition of CIP as concluded from the UV–vis spectra. The loading ratio and cumulative release ratio were calculated via the following equations:
$$
\mathrm{Loading~ratio(\%)}{=}\frac{\mathsf{A}_{0}\ {-}\mathsf{A}_{\mathrm{t}1}}{\mathsf{A}_{0}}\mathrm{X}100\%
$$
Cumulative release $\mathrm{ratio}(\%){=}\frac{\mathsf{A}_{\mathrm{t}2}{-}\mathsf{A}_{1}}{\mathsf{A}_{0}{-}\mathsf{A}_{1}}\mathrm{X}100\%$
$\mathsf{A}_{0}$ : the absorption intensity of initial CIP solution; $\mathsf{A}_{\mathrm{t}1}$ : the absorption intensity of the supernatant solution at time t; $\mathsf{A}_{\mathsf{t}2}$ : the absorption intensity of supernatant solution at time t after light illumination;
$\mathsf{A}_{1}$ : the absorption intensity of the supernatant solution at the adsorption–desorption equilibrium.
## 2.4. Characterization
Crystal structures of the samples were characterized by X-ray difraction (XRD) recorded on a Bruker AXS D8 diffractometer using Cu $\mathsf{K}\mathsf{\P}$ radiation. Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectra were obtained on a Bruker ALPHA-T spectrometer using KBr pellets. Thermogravimetric (TG) analysis was performed on a TA SDT Q600 integration thermal analyzer with a heating rate of $5~^{\circ}C/$ min under air atmosphere. Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area and pore-size distribution of the samples were measured by a Micromeritics ASAP Kubo X1000 analyzer at liquid nitrogen temperature. The diffuse reflectance spectra (DRS) of the samples were obtained using a UV-2600 Shimadzu UV–Vis spectrophotometer. Scanning electron microscope (SEM) and transmission electron microscope (TEM) were performed using Hitachi S-4800 and JEOL JEM-2100F to analyze the specific crystal structure information.
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurements were performed on a Thermo Fisher Scientifc Escalab 250 spectrometer using the C 1s line at $284.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ to calibrate the peak positions.
## 2.5. Theoretical calculations
The DFT calculations were carried out using the Vienna Abinitio Simulation Package (VASP) [41,42] with the frozen-core allelectron projector-augment-wave (PAW) [43,44] method. The Perdew-Burke-Ernzerhof (PBE) [45] of generalized gradient approximation (GGA) was adopted to describe the exchange and correlation potential. The cutoff energy for the plane-wave basis set was set to $450~\mathrm{eV}$ . The primitive cell of $\mathrm{C}_{28}\mathrm{Bi}_{4}\mathrm{O}_{26}$ was used and the Monkhorst-Pack $\mathbf{k}$ -point [46] sampling was set to $1\times1\times3$ . The geometry optimizations were performed until the forces on each ion was reduced below $0.01\ \mathrm{eV}/{\mathring{A}}.$ The resulting structures were then used to calculate the electronic structures.
## 3. Results and discussion
The prepared SU-101 was characterized by XRD, FT-IR, and TGA spectra (Fig. S1), all of which are consistent with those previously reported in the literature [37], suggesting the successful synthesis of SU-101. In addition, as can be seen from Fig. S2 and S3, SU-101 exhibits a columnar like morphology with a diameter of $50\pm20~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and the length varies greatly from 80 to $260~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . The nano-columns are easy to aggregate. Compared with pure SU101, the XRD peaks of CIP@SU-101 decrease significantly (Fig. 1a), which is attributed to the loading of CIP, as adsorbed CIP on the surface may block the X ray absorption of SU-101, resulting in decreased diffraction intensity. Furthermore, FT-IR spectra (Fig. 1b) were also carried out to check the CIP loading onto
SU-101. Compared with pristine SU-101, the intensity of the d $({\mathsf{C}}{=}0)$ stretching vibration and the $\delta(-\mathsf{O H})$ stretching vibration for CIP@SU-101 obviously increase [47]. Besides that, a new peak at $1630~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (attributed to the $\mathsf{v}(-\mathsf{N}{-}\mathsf{H})$ bending vibration for CIP) appears for CIP@SU-101 [47]. These differences suggest the presence of CIP in $\mathrm{CIP}@\mathrm{SU-}101$ . In addition, after loading of CIP, the peak of –OH group shifts from $3528~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for pristine CIP to $3422~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and the peak of $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ group shifts from $1705~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for pristine SU-101 to $1703~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which may be induced by the formation of hydrogen bond between CIP and SU-101, specifically the –OH group in CIP and the $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ group in SU-101. (Fig. 1c)
TGA was further used to confirm that drug molecules are chemically adsorbed on the surface of SU-101 via hydrogen bond. Concerning that the fluorine containing CIP is harmful to the instrument, we choose another drugs, i.e. doxorubicin hydrochloride (DOX) to compare the heat induced collapse behaviour. As presented in Fig. 1d, DOX begins to decompose at about $197^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ while for $\mathsf{D O X@S U-101}$ , no weight loss can be observed. However, the weight loss for DOX $@$ SU-101 $(40\%)$ at $380^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ is larger than that for pure SU-101 $(37\%)$ , suggesting the presence of DOX for DOX@SU-101. The TGA results suggest that the decomposition temperature for both DOX and SU-101 in $\mathsf{D O X@S U-101}$ increase, which is due to the chemical interaction between DOX and SU101, specifically, hydrogen bond. TET displays similar hydrogen bond with SU-101, as can be seen in Fig. S4.
Besides FT-IR and TGA results, adjusting the pH of the suspension also confirms the presence of hydrogen bond between CIP and SU-101. It is well known that highly acidic and basic environments can break hydrogen bonds. Therefore, hydrochloric acid and sodium hydroxide were used to adjust the pH of the suspension containing SU-101 and CIP. As expected, it is found that under either acidic or basic condition, the CIP adsorption capacity over SU-101 is far lower than that without adjusting the pH. As is shown in Fig. 1e, the loading ratio is only $10\%$ when the pH is 1.74, and it is $1.6\%$ at $\mathsf{p H}=11.3$ . These results suggest that the adsorption capacity significantly decreases once the hydrogen bond is destroyed, and hydrogen bond plays an important role in the adsorption capacity of SU-101.
The effect of CIP concentration on the loading ratio was further studied. As shown in Fig. 2a, at the beginning, the loading ratio increases with increasing of the concentration of CIP. When the adsorption–desorption process reaches a balance, the loading ratio decreases with the increase of CIP concentration. The maximum CIP loading ratio for SU-101 is $85.8\%$ at the concentration of $10\mathrm{mg/L}$ . Therefore, a concentration of $10\mathrm{mg/L}$ was used in the following CIP release experiment.
The adsorption kinetics, as described by the relationship between loading ratio and time, are presented in Fig. 2b. It is found that the loading ratio of CIP over SU-101 increases quickly in the first hour, and after three hours, the adsorption–desorption process almost reaches equilibrium. Pseudo-first-order and pseudosecond-order were further used to reveal the adsorption kinetics. The pseudo-first-order (Eq. (1)) and the pseudo-second-order equation (Eq. (2)) are given as: [48]
$$
\mathrm{lg(Q_{e}{-}Q_{t})}{=}~\mathrm{lg}\mathrm{Q_{e}{-}}\frac{\mathrm{K}_{1}}{2.303}\mathrm{t}
$$
$$
\frac{t}{\mathsf{Q}_{\mathrm{t}}}{=}\frac{1}{\mathsf{K}_{2}\mathsf{Q}_{\mathrm{e}}^{2}}{+}\frac{\mathsf{t}}{\mathsf{Q}_{\mathrm{e}}}
$$
$\mathsf{Q}_{\mathrm{e}}\left(\mathrm{mg}/\mathrm{g}\right)$ :the loading ratio at equilibrium;   
$\sf{Q}_{t}\left(\mathrm{mg}/\mathrm{g}\right)$ : the loading ratio at time $\operatorname{t}(\mathrm{h})$ ;   
$\ensuremath{\mathrm{K}}_{1}(\ensuremath{\mathrm{h}}^{-1})$ :the first-order rate constant;   
$\mathsf{K}_{2}\left(\mathsf{g/m g}\right)$ :the second-order rate constant.
The fitting parameters are shown in Table 1. Obviously, the adsorption kinetics fit pseudo-second-order better than the pseudo-first-order (Fig. S5), indicating that the adsorption capacity of CIP is proportional to the number of active sites on the surface of SU-101 and the rate-limiting step is chemisorption [35], which are consistent with the conclusion that hydrogen bond is present for CIP@SU-101 deduced from the FT-IR results.
It was further discovered that light could cause the release of CIP mentioned above. As shown in Fig. 3a, the CIP concentration shows an apparent increase upon light irradiation. The amount of released CIP is dependent on the light wavelength. $95.56\%$ CIP is released under the irradiation of full spectrum light (Fig. 3b). Under visible light irradiation, the released CIP decreases to be $36.93\%$ , and few CIP is released upon near-infrared light irradiation. It is obvious that this behavior coincides with the light absorption of SU-101. As can be seen from the DRS spectra (Fig. 3c), SU-101 exhibits strong absorption between 300 and $450~\mathrm{nm}$ (ranges from UV light to small part of visible light), and SU-101 hardly absorbs any infrared light. Control experiments in the absence of either light or SU-101 suggest that no CIP can be released (Fig. 3d). This result demonstrates that the release of CIP from CIP@SU-101 is a light induced process.
To confirm the conclusion that the photo-triggered release is driven by the light absorption of SU-101, a more detailed experiment using monochromatic light with different wavelengths was carried out. As expected, the CIP release ratio is $10.53\%$ , $11.08\%$ , $4.987\%$ , $1.673\%$ , $1.243\%$ , $1.233\%$ , $0.306\%$ and $0.402\%$ when the wavelengths are $365\ \mathrm{nm}$ , $385\ \mathrm{nm}$ , $420\ \mathrm{nm}$ , $450\ \mathrm{nm}$ , $485\ \mathrm{nm}$ , $520~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , $595~\mathrm{nm}$ and $630~\mathrm{{nm}}$ respectively (Fig. 3c), and nearly no CIP is released upon light illumination with the wavelength of $595\mathrm{nm}$ and $630~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . This action is nearly the same as the absorption of SU-101, further confirming that the light absorption of SU101 is responsible for the release.
In addition, to testify the universal applicability, other common drugs, such as AMO, TET, NOR and DOX, were also investigated to evaluate the drug loading performance of SU-101. As shown in Fig. 4a, SU-101 displays large loading capability of different drugs, especially NOR $(91\%)$ , and DOX $(91\%)$ which makes it an ideal candidate for drug carrier. Light-triggered drug release for these common drugs were further investigated. As is shown in Fig. 4b, cumulative release ratio of NOR, AMO, TET and DOX reach to approximately $93.45\ \%$ , $100\%$ , $100\%$ , and $78\%$ respectively. These results are understandable considering the fact that all the mentioned drugs possess –OH groups capable of forming hydrogen bond (Fig. 4c), which also suggests the universal application of
SU-101 as a potential candidate for drug loading and light triggered release.
In order to find the mechanism of the light-triggered drug release over SU-101, the difference before and after light irradiation for SU-101 was checked. Surprisingly, the color of SU-101 powder is gradually getting darker upon UV or visible light irradiation (Fig. 5a) (the sample after light irradiation is denoted as SU101-L). Compared with SU-101, SU-101-L exhibits a wider absorption range according to the DRS spectra (Fig. 5b). When SU-101-L is kept in the air at room temperature for 2 days (the sample is denoted as SU-101-L-R), the color returns back and the DRS spectrum recovers to the same as that for SU-101. In other words, SU-101 displays a reversible photochromic property. It is worth noting that SU-101 is the first reported photochromic bismuth metal–organic frameworks, to the best of our knowledge, though several bismuth based inorganic compounds were reported as photochromic materials [49-51].
The photochromism of SU-101 is also dependent on light wavelength. As presented in Fig. S7, when the SU-101 suspension in water is exposed to monochromatic light with different wavelengths, the light with wavelength of $365~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and $385~\mathrm{{nm}}$ induce the strongest color change. The color change becomes gradually weak with increasing the wavelength and the color hardly changes with the irradiation of $630\ \mathrm{nm}$ wavelength. Again, this color change coincides with the light response of SU-101 too, which is highly assumed to be closely related to the light-triggered drug release.
Structure transition or phase change are often associated with color changes in other known photochromic Bi based inorganic compounds [49,51]. Therefore, XRD of SU-101, SU-101-L and SU101-L-R were carefully investigated and compared. Interestingly, no distinguishable changes can be observed for SU-101, SU-101-L and SU-101-L-R, suggesting the crystal structure does not change during the photochromic process. (Fig. 5c) In addition, XPS (Fig. 5d), SEM (Fig. S2) and TEM (Fig. S3) altogether suggest that SU-101 is stable during the photochromic process, in terms of both chemical state and morphology.
The bond information for SU-101, SU-101-L and SU-101-L-R were also investigated using FT-IR. As can be seen from Fig. 5e, most of the peaks are nearly the same for the three samples, except the peak for the stretching vibration of inner ester group $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ bond [52], which shifts from 1705 to $1712~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ after light irradiation. Combined with the crystal structure of SU-101 (Fig. 1c and Scheme 1), this shift is highly attributed to the broken of hydrogen bond between $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and the O of $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ group. That is to say, hydrogen bond between $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and the O in the $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ group could be broken under light illumination. The presence of hydrogen bond echoes the FT-IR spectra for CIP@SU-101, which also suggests the presence of hydrogen bond between the $-{\mathsf{O H}}$ group in CIP and the O in the $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ group in SU-101. Furthermore, the light induced hydrogen bond broken may be the reason for the drug release of SU-101.
$\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms for SU-101, SU-101-L and SU-101-L-R are presented in Fig. S6, and the calculated values are listed in Table 2, which supports above conclusion that light irradiation broken the hydrogen bond. SU-101 displays a pore size of $10.38~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , which is smaller for SU-101-L $14.08~\mathrm{nm}^{\cdot}$ . Light irradiation breaks the hydrogen bond between $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and the O in the $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ group, and then water molecules are removed from the channels of SU-101, which causes the bigger pore diameter of SU-101-L than SU-101. When the color returns back, the hydrogen bond between $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and the O in the $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ group is re-established, which causes that the pore size decreases again $(9.32\ \mathrm{nm})$ . The BET surface area displays similar trend, namely SU-101-L $(232\ m^{2}/{\bf g})\ >\ {\bf S}{\bf U}{-}101$ $(150~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})\approx\mathrm{SU}-101\mathrm{-L}\mathrm{-R}~(1\$ $(126~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ .
Total density of states (TDOS) and partial density of states (PDOS) were further calculated to clearly reveal the contribution of each atom to the electronic structure. From the TDOS diagram, the calculated band gap of SU-101 is $1.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ , smaller than the experimental value of $2.6~\mathrm{eV}.$ This is a common phenomenon for DFT calculation owning to the well-known underestimation of the band gap. It is found that Bi atoms barely contribute to the frontier orbitals while the broad valence and conduction band are both mainly composed of O and C atoms in the organic ligand (Fig. 6a). In addition, after a careful examination of the PDOS for the different oxygen atoms, it is suggested that O3 (O in $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ , Scheme 1) only contributes to the valence band, while the other oxygen atoms O2 and O4 contribute not only the valence band but also the conduction band, and O1 and O5 display little contribution to either the conduction band or valence band (Fig. 6b and Scheme 1). This means that upon light irradiation, the electron density for O3 would decrease, which is the reason for the broken of the hydrogen bond.
In short, drugs containing –OH group adsorb onto SU-101 with the formation of hydrogen bond between the $-\mathsf{O H}$ and the $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ group. Light irradiation with the wavelength shorter than the band gap of SU-101 decreases the electron density around the $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ group, which consequently breaks the hydrogen bond and results in the drug release. Therefore, the possible mechanism for the light-triggered drug release over SU-101 is proposed and summarized in Scheme 1.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, Bi based MOFs SU-101 is found to be an ideal drug carrier. SU-101 displays efficient drug loading capacity and the amount of drug released could be controlled by changing the wavelength of light and the irradiation time. The mechanism of light-triggered drug release is believed to be closely related to the broken of hydrogen bond between drug molecules and SU101 caused by light illumination. Moreover, the loading and release properties of SU-101 are also found be universal to other drugs which can be able to form hydrogen bond. This work suggests that Bi based MOFs SU-101 hold great potential in drug loading and light triggered release, which may provide a new alternative for light triggered drug carrier.
